# Exercise: Building a Path Under Home
#
# A common pattern: stick a config file under the user's home
# directory, falling back to `/tmp` if for some reason home isn't
# available.
#
# Two pieces:
#
#   os.home-dir                       ( -- String Bool )
#   "/etc" "hosts" os.path-join       ( String String -- String )
#
# `os.home-dir` has the same shape as `os.getenv` from exercise
# 01: it returns the home directory path and a Bool that says
# whether home was actually available — true means the path on
# the stack is real, false means the lookup failed and the path
# is "". Branch on it the same way.
#
# `os.path-join` returns just the joined string, so no branching
# needed there.
#
# Your task: write `home-file` — given a filename, build a full
# path by joining the user's home directory (or `/tmp` if home is
# unavailable) with the filename.
#
# Examples:
#
#   "config.toml" home-file   → "/home/alice/config.toml"
#   "notes.txt"   home-file   → "/home/alice/notes.txt"
#
# Stack hint: after `os.home-dir` your stack is
# ( filename home Bool ). The `if` consumes the Bool, leaving
# ( filename home ). On the true branch: leave it alone — `home`
# is what you want. On the false branch: replace `home` with
# "/tmp". Then `swap os.path-join` produces the final path.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: home-file ( String -- String )
    # Your code here
    drop ""
;

: test-contains-name ( -- )
    "app.toml" home-file
    "app.toml" string.contains

    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
